lesson physics-streamlined-shapes v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Streamlined shapes"
  goal "Measure how fast shapes sink through water and explain why a streamlined shape meets the least water resistance."

  look {
    scene 36x17 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        droptube tb shapes=["ball", "flat", "pointed", "cube"] time=2 grams=[20, 20, 20, 20]
        narrowboat b windows=4 pots=2
      }
    }
    say """
      Anything moving through water has to push the water out of its way, and the water pushes back.
      That push is called water resistance. Four lumps of clay, each weighing 20 g, were let go
      together in tubes of canal water. After two seconds the pointed one has sunk furthest and the
      flat one the least, because a flat face has to shove the most water aside. A shape that slips
      through with little push is streamlined, like a fish, a duck or the pointed front of a boat.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.drop-fastest count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    level easy {
      worked physics.drop-read w=2 t=2 level=medium
    }
    practice physics.drop-read count=2 seed=2 easy-seed=3 hard-seed=4
    practice physics.drop-why-flat count=1 seed=1
    practice physics.drop-bottom count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=5 hard-seed=2
    practice physics.drop-fastest count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=7 hard-seed=8
    level hard {
      show physics.drop-read w=2 t=2 level=medium
    }
  }
  story {
    show physics.canal-bows p=0 easy-p=1 hard-p=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.drop-fair w=1 heavy=30 easy-w=2 easy-heavy=40 hard-w=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.drop-together v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Water pushes back on anything moving through it, and least on a streamlined shape."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Children often think the heaviest thing always wins, so every lump in these tests weighs the
    same and only the shape changes; the two-star question has one lump that is heavier and asks
    which tube spoils the test. Ask for the reason in the child's own words: the flat shape has to
    push the most water out of the way. The speeds are read off the scale in centimetres a second,
    and the time to the bottom is the depth divided by the speed, which is the same sharing as in
    maths. If the reading of the scale is shaky, stay with the first two questions for another day.
    Off the page, fill a tall jar or a vase with water, press the same piece of modelling clay into
    a ball, a flat pancake and a point, and time each to the bottom. In the three-star question the
    slower shape is let go first, and the gap is the difference between the two times. The speeds on
    the drawings are the lesson's round numbers, chosen to keep the order and the sharing clear, not
    measurements: a real 20 g lump falls faster than this in water, and a flat pancake tends to
    tumble. A tall jar of wallpaper paste or cooking oil slows every shape enough to see the order.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the shapes to predict include the pointed one or the flat one, a worked example reads a speed first, the drawings show one second, the odd lump weighs twice as much, and the two shapes to arrive together are a ball and a pointed one."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the shapes to predict are closer in speed, the drawings show two seconds so a speed has to be shared, the heavy lump is the pointed one, which would win anyway, and the shapes to arrive together are the flat one and the pointed one."
  }
}
